":"Song of Solomon*"},{"id":"Woman","title":"Woman"}],"dictionaryWords":[{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Damascus","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Damascus::Damascus::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Damascus"},"text":"Your neck is like a tower made of ivory; your eyes are like the pools of Heshbon by the gate of Bath-rabbim; your nose is like the tower of Lebanon, facing toward Damascus.","verse":4}],"id":"Damascus","label":"Damascus","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Damascus::Damascus::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Damascus","term":"Damascus"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Damascus","sourceTitle":"Damascus"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":["Beauty in Nature"],"sourceClass":"theme-title","t":"h"},{"c":["At the end of God’s work of Creation, God declared that everything he had made was “very good” (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.1.31","r":"Gen.1.31","rawR":"Gen.1.31","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.1.31","value":"Gen.1.31"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gen 1:31"},"). The beauty and wonder of God’s creation is often reflected on in Scripture, particularly in the psalms (see, e.g., ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.8.3-4","r":"Ps.8.3-4","rawR":"Ps.8.3-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.8.3-4","value":"Ps.8.3-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 8:3-4"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.104.1-35","r":"Ps.104.1-35","rawR":"Ps.104.1-35","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.104.1-35","value":"Ps.104.1-35"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 104:1-35"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body-fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Nature—and imagery from nature—also features prominently in the Song of Songs. The man and woman in the Song often express sexual intimacy in the countryside (",{"href":"?bref=Song.2.8-17","r":"Song.2.8-17","rawR":"Song.2.8-17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.2.8-17","value":"Song.2.8-17"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:8-17"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Song.4.12-5.1","r":"Song.4.12-5.1","rawR":"Song.4.12-5.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.4.12-5.1","value":"Song.4.12-5.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:12–5:1"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Song.6.2-3","r":"Song.6.2-3","rawR":"Song.6.2-3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.2-3","value":"Song.6.2-3"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:2-3"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Song.6.11-12","r":"Song.6.11-12","rawR":"Song.6.11-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.11-12","value":"Song.6.11-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"11-12"},"), a place of pleasant fragrances and secluded meeting places. The garden, vineyard, and orchard create intimate and pleasant settings and evoke a romantic mood. The mystique of nature provides them with the environment in which their love is the most rustic, and at the same time the most exotic and exhilarating. Additionally, the couple uses extensive metaphors from nature to describe each other’s physical attributes and their arousing effects. Their portrayal of each other leads them to use illustrations from God’"],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Nature points people to the Creator, as it contains evidence of his power, goodness, justice, and love (see ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.19.1-4","r":"Ps.19.1-4","rawR":"Ps.19.1-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.19.1-4","value":"Ps.19.1-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 19:1-4"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Rom.1.18-20","r":"Rom.1.18-20","rawR":"Rom.1.18-20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Rom.1.18-20","value":"Rom.1.18-20"}],"t":"ref","v":"Rom 1:18-20"},"). God’s penchant for redemption, restoration, and new creation is also evidenced in many ways through the beauty of nature—for instance, in the changing of seasons, the growth of plants, and the birth of children."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Passages for Further Study"],"sourceClass":"theme-refs-title","t":"h"},{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Gen.2.1-9","r":"Gen.2.1-9","rawR":"Gen.2.1-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.2.1-9","value":"Gen.2.1-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gen 2:1-9"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.8.3-8","r":"Ps.8.3-8","rawR":"Ps.8.3-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.8.3-8","value":"Ps.8.3-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Pss 8:3-8"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.19.1-6","r":"Ps.19.1-6","rawR":"Ps.19.1-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.19.1-6","value":"Ps.19.1-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:1-6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.96.5-6","r":"Ps.96.5-6","rawR":"Ps.96.5-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.96.5-6","value":"Ps.96.5-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"96:5-6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Song.2.8-17","r":"Song.2.8-17","rawR":"Song.2.8-17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.2.8-17","value":"Song.2.8-17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Song 2:8-17"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Song.4.12-5.1","r":"Song.4.12-5.1","rawR":"Song.4.12-5.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.4.12-5.1","value":"Song.4.12-5.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:12–5:1"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Song.6.2-3","r":"Song.6.2-3","rawR":"Song.6.2-3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.2-3","value":"Song.6.2-3"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:2-3"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Song.6.11-12","r":"Song.6.11-12","rawR":"Song.6.11-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.11-12","value":"Song.6.11-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"11-12"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Song.7.10-13","r":"Song.7.10-13","rawR":"Song.7.10-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.7.10-13","value":"Song.7.10-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:10-13"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.6.26-30","r":"Matt.6.26-30","rawR":"Matt.6.26-30","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.6.26-30","value":"Matt.6.26-30"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 6:26-30"}],"sourceClass":"theme-refs","t":"p"}],"id":"ThemeNote:BeautyInNature","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":7,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":7,"startVerse":10}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"BeautyInNature","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"ThemeNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[7],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"b93a0f7eb978a4ae50ebb968a5910019f3323f9b6c271be494cb5f2eb6ad5ab9","sourceItemName":"BeautyInNature","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/ThemeNotes.xml","sourceOrder":138,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.7.10-13","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":"Beauty in Nature","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Beauty in Nature","type":"ThemeNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.6.13-7.9","r":"Song.6.13-7.9","rawR":"Song.6.13-7.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.13-7.9","value":"Song.6.13-7.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:13–7:9"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," An interaction between the young women of Jerusalem and the man introduces his last major description of the woman’s beauty and his desire to hold her (see ",{"href":"?item=Song.4.1-5.1_StudyNote_Filament","id":"Song.4.1-5.1_StudyNote_Filament","rawId":"Song.4.1-5.1_StudyNote_Filament","t":"link","v":"study note on 4:1–5:1"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.6.13-7.9","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":7,"endVerse":9,"startChapter":6,"startVerse":13}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.6.13-7.9","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[6,7],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"b09afae5b2978df503f13f720eacae74ef25b4652f75c0b300622f65447e4d30","sourceItemName":"Song.6.13-7.9","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8176,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.6.13-7.9","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.6.13-7.9","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.7.1-5","r":"Song.7.1-5","rawR":"Song.7.1-5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.7.1-5","value":"Song.7.1-5"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:1-5"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The man now praises the woman’s dancing ",{"c":["feet"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," and then ascends her sensuous body to her flowing ",{"c":["hair"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (",{"href":"?bref=Song.7.1-5","r":"Song.7.1-5","rawR":"Song.7.1-5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.7.1-5","value":"Song.7.1-5"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:1-5"},"). • This ",{"c":["queenly maiden"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (literally ",{"c":["prince’s daughter"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},") might or might not be from a royal family, but the man considers her as fine as royalty."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.7.1-5","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":7,"endVerse":5,"startChapter":7,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.7.1-5","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[7],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"3efb5c863300fafc964910d393cd5c630458d9f11838e454b071f1e1759d5ccf","sourceItemName":"Song.7.1-5","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8178,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.7.1-5","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.7.1-5","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.7.2","r":"Song.7.2","rawR":"Song.7.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.7.2","value":"Song.7.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:2"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Between your thighs lies a mound of wheat"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (literally ",{"c":["Your belly is like a mound of wheat"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},"): The man is either describing the woman’s smoothly rounded abdomen or her pubic mound."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.7.2","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":7,"endVerse":2,"startChapter":7,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.7.2","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[7],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"3b973f3b01d027dfa051a900e8c7acc99db72bb92e79e3d931dff8c09ee9c8e8","sourceItemName":"Song.7.2","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8179,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.7.2","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.7.2","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.7.4","r":"Song.7.4","rawR":"Song.7.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.7.4","value":"Song.7.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["sparkling pools in Heshbon by the gate of Bath-rabbim:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The pools of Heshbon, formed by a brook, can still be seen today near the ruins of Heshbon on the east coast of the Dead Sea. • The ",{"c":["tower of Lebanon"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," is probably a metaphor for Mount Hermon, a high mountain that overlooks the valley of ",{"c":["Damascus"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," the capital of Syria. The majestic beauty of the woman’s nose rises above her other facial features."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.7.4","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":7,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":7,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.7.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[7],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"34761a3386e6c57915ad5fdf645beccb28ee6c6486328a220dac65464a0d5894","sourceItemName":"Song.7.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8180,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.7.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.7.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.7.5-6","r":"Song.7.5-6","rawR":"Song.7.5-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.7.5-6","value":"Song.7.5-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:5-6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Mount Carmel"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," is a high mountain range on the Mediterranean coast. • ",{"c":["your hair radiates royalty"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (literally ",{"c":["your"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["hair is as purple threads"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},"): Purple was an expensive color to produce, so it was the exclusive property of royalty."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.7.5-6","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":7,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":7,"startVerse":5}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.7.5-6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[7],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"48b375e5b391914fd8b20cfb216cbdbee1f4a65ab75793d232db200487490a1d","sourceItemName":"Song.7.5-6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8181,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.7.5-6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.7.5-6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.7.7-8","r":"Song.7.7-8","rawR":"Song.7.7-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.7.7-8","value":"Song.7.7-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:7-8"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The husband describes their lovemaking. He ascends her body and caresses her ",{"c":["breasts"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.7.7-8","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":7,"endVerse":8,"startChapter":7,"startVerse":7}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.7.7-8","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[7],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"a813ed49525dacab3ed90e7efb10c2237556665259e7f13680976cdc40ea657c","sourceItemName":"Song.7.7-8","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8182,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.7.7-8","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.7.7-8","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.7.9","r":"Song.7.9","rawR":"Song.7.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.7.9","value":"Song.7.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:9"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The man compares the woman’s love and her ",{"c":["kisses"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," to ",{"c":["wine"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," in that they are delightful and make one light-headed (see ",{"href":"?bref=Song.1.2","r":"Song.1.2","rawR":"Song.1.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.1.2","value":"Song.1.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:2"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.7.9","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":7,"endVerse":9,"startChapter":7,"startVerse":9}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.7.9","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[7],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"0c05a0173fb1e08f9b3c4e151f0e27f2fd5089787ad39309dff916ceb00fed96","sourceItemName":"Song.7.9","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8183,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.7.9","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.7.9","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.7.11-12","r":"Song.7.11-12","rawR":"Song.7.11-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.7.11-12","value":"Song.7.11-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:11-12"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The woman suggests that they go together to check on the status of the vineyards and make love while there."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.7.11-12","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":7,"endVerse":12,"startChapter":7,"startVerse":11}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.7.11-12","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[7],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"0d0bd23a40159ce40924a706f1e8b4f9dadbd28ae1e00e9bb010ffc1648f617a","sourceItemName":"Song.7.11-12","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8184,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.7.11-12","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.7.11-12","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.7.13","r":"Song.7.13","rawR":"Song.7.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.7.13","value":"Song.7.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:13"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Mandrakes"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (Hebrew ",{"c":["duda’im"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"},") were a Middle Eastern aphrodisiac (see ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.30.14-16","r":"Gen.30.14-16","rawR":"Gen.30.14-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.30.14-16","value":"Gen.30.14-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gen 30:14-16"},"); the roots of the plant resemble a human torso. The word is a play on ",{"c":["my lover"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (Hebrew ",{"c":["dodi"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"},"). • ",{"c":["at our door:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," All sexual pleasures are available to them now, and she will share with him the ",{"c":["delights"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," she has ",{"c":["saved"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," for him alone. • ",{"c":["new . . . old:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," She has in mind the sexual expressions they have already experienced, and new ones as well."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.7.13","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":7,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":7,"startVerse":13}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.7.13","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[7],"sourceItemFragme